WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Multi-camera AI monitoring is an architecture problem, not just a model problem.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:18.680
The system includes camera placement, RTSP streams, NVRs, networks, edge or central compute, models, storage, event pipelines, dashboards, alerting, logs, and human review.

3
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:31.520
Centralized, edge, hybrid, NVR-integrated, batch, and near-real-time patterns trade simplicity, cost, latency, privacy, resilience, and scale.

4
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:42.020
Camera resolution, frame rate, substreams, lighting, sampling, model size, and hardware determine both performance and event quality.

5
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:51.500
Staged pipelines can use motion, object detection, tracking, descriptions, and vision-language review to reduce cost and noise.

6
00:00:51.600 --> 00:01:00.960
Every stage introduces false-positive and false-negative risk, so versions, thresholds, local evaluation, and visible degradation matter.

7
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:07.560
Retention and access policy should minimize unnecessary video and metadata while preserving authorized event review.

8
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:15.500
Alerts need grouping, clear routing, useful context, and human confirmation before consequential action.

9
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:28.340
Strong designs secure credentials and remote access, segment networks, harden servers, audit video use, monitor pipeline health, and recover configuration reliably.

10
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:38.760
Local-first processing can improve control and privacy, but only disciplined governance, careful tuning, and accountable human oversight make the architecture trustworthy.
